 \begin{abstract}
-Microbial communities in hyperarid soils such as those of the Rub' al-Khali persist through oxidation of atmospheric $\Htwo$ and CO, a thermally buffered horizon 5--10 cm below the surface, and rare moisture pulses. Rainfall enhancement, revegetation, brine disposal and land use in these deserts are planned without models that predict microbial responses, keep hierarchical quantities such as total hydrogenase capacity consistent with their subclasses, and make the assumptions behind each scenario explicit. We developed EQ-BioDT, a digital twin that integrates \nWGS{} shotgun metagenomes, X-ray fluorescence geochemistry, 1,227 primary-site 16S rRNA amplicon profiles and climate data from \nSites{} sites along a 1,043 km transect into a cube of \nStates{} site-compartment states. EQ-BioDT has three layers: statistical predictors validated on held-out sites and campaigns, a constitutive layer that projects predictions onto linear constraints compiled from the axioms of an OWL 2 ontology, and six mechanistic scenario engines calibrated from the literature and from paired field contrasts. Under spatially blocked cross-validation on \nGuildWGS{} metagenomes from \nWGSsites{} sites, EQ-BioDT predicted group 1l [NiFe]-hydrogenase abundance with Spearman $\rho = 0.543$ (site-clustered 95\% CI 0.470--0.622), compared with 0.347 for a compartment-only and 0.243 for a climate-only model, and 16S Shannon diversity of the 180 states with $\rho = 0.547$ (0.404--0.655). In a retrospective temporal holdout trained on the first four of five expeditions, it predicted group 1l abundance in the fifth with $\rho = 0.619$, compared with 0.552 for site-compartment persistence. Projection onto the constraint set removed every hierarchical violation and reduced mean squared error by 3.51\% (2.11--5.10\%), within 0.2\% of the root mean squared error of covariance-weighted reconciliation and below that of bottom-up summation and matched-feature ridge regression. The revegetation and refugium engines reproduced the paired root-versus-bulk and surface-versus-subsurface contrasts within their empirical confidence intervals. In scenario simulations at an interdune site, a 6 mm rainfall pulse raised Shannon diversity by 0.35 and retained 76\% of the minimum $\Htwo$ oxidation power, whereas a 25 mm pulse retained 20\%; 30\% shrub cover lowered group 1l capacity per genome equivalent while raising its absolute density 1.38-fold. Across the 92,160 km$^2$ empirically supported corridor, four-pathway carbon accounting aligned with seven cross-desert plausibility comparisons and estimated 811.3 kt $\COtwoeq$ $\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$ of gross baseline binding (140.9 kt $\COtwo$ $\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$ in persistent soil organic and pedogenic carbonate pools; 1.50 Mt $\COtwoeq$ $\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$ gross across the 650,000 km$^2$ basin), rising to 8.42 Mt $\COtwoeq$ $\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$ gross (1.31 Mt $\COtwo$ $\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$ persistent) under corridor eco-restoration and falling to 0.13 Mt $\COtwoeq$ $\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$ under unmitigated disturbance. EQ-BioDT provides validated predictions of desert trace-gas guilds and auditable, assumption-explicit scenarios for dryland management.
