 \section{Introduction}
 
-Hyperarid deserts receive too little rain to sustain photosynthetic primary production for most of the year, and their soil microbial communities persist on alternative energy sources and physical shelter. Aerobic bacteria in these soils oxidize atmospheric $\Htwo$ and CO at trace concentrations using high-affinity [NiFe]-hydrogenases and form I carbon monoxide dehydrogenases, and the energy from this oxidation supports survival and, in some lineages, carbon fixation \cite{bay2021trace,grinter2023structural,leung2020energetic}. Across aridity gradients, the contribution of these chemosynthetic guilds to primary production increases as photosynthetic contributions decline \cite{bay2021aridity,ji2017atmospheric,leung2025aridity}. Soil $\Htwo$ oxidation also removes an indirect greenhouse gas by preserving tropospheric hydroxyl radicals that oxidize methane \cite{ehhalt2009h2cycle,ocko2022hydrogen,paulot2024reanalysis}, while alkaline desert soils precipitate pedogenic carbonate \cite{schlesinger1985caliche,ma2013inorganic}. The topmost centimetres of sand reach surface temperatures above $58\degC$ and receive intense ultraviolet radiation, whereas the horizon 5--10 cm below the surface remains cooler and shelters a distinct community. Hydration events resuscitate dormant cells within days \cite{jordaan2020gobi,imminger2024resuscitation}, and extracellular relic DNA, which persists after cell death, inflates diversity estimates obtained from total soil DNA \cite{carini2016relicdna,lennon2018relicdna}. In inland and coastal sabkhas, evaporite minerals add a geochemical axis: calcium sulfate and sodium chloride brines impose osmotic stress of very different severity.
+Hyperarid deserts receive too little rain to sustain photosynthetic primary production for most of the year, and their soil microbial communities persist on alternative energy sources and physical shelter. Aerobic bacteria in these soils oxidize atmospheric $\Htwo$ and CO at trace concentrations using high-affinity [NiFe]-hydrogenases and form I carbon monoxide dehydrogenases, and the energy from this oxidation supports survival and, in some lineages, carbon fixation \cite{bay2021trace,grinter2023structural,leung2020energetic}. Across aridity gradients, the contribution of these chemosynthetic guilds to primary production increases as photosynthetic contributions decline \cite{bay2021aridity,ji2017atmospheric,leung2025aridity}. Soil $\Htwo$ oxidation also removes an indirect greenhouse gas by preserving tropospheric hydroxyl radicals that oxidize methane \cite{ehhalt2009h2cycle,ocko2022hydrogen,paulot2024reanalysis}, while alkaline desert soils precipitate pedogenic carbonate \cite{schlesinger1985caliche,ma2013inorganic}. The topmost centimetres of sand reach surface temperatures above $58\degC$ and receive intense ultraviolet radiation, whereas the horizon 5--10 cm below the surface remains cooler and shelters a distinct community. Hydration events can resuscitate dormant cells within minutes \cite{jordaan2020gobi,imminger2024resuscitation}, and extracellular relic DNA, which persists after cell death, inflates diversity estimates obtained from total soil DNA \cite{carini2016relicdna,lennon2018relicdna}. In inland and coastal sabkhas, evaporite minerals add a geochemical axis: calcium sulfate and sodium chloride brines impose osmotic stress of very different severity.
 
 These mechanisms determine how desert soils respond to the environmental interventions now planned for the Arabian Peninsula. Rainfall enhancement adds water, but water also fills the pores through which atmospheric gases reach the cells that oxidize them; gas diffusivity in soil falls steeply with water-filled pore space \cite{millington1961permeability}. Revegetation creates root zones with more microbial biomass, but root exudates favour heterotrophs over trace-gas scavengers. Desalination plants discharge brines whose ionic composition differs from that of natural sabkha waters, and reintroduced ungulates, livestock and seismic surveys compact the surface layer and strip the subsurface refugium. Each intervention acts on several of these coupled mechanisms at once, so its outcome depends on how moisture, gas transport, temperature, salinity and community composition interact at a given site.
 
 Current approaches address parts of this problem. Correlative models fitted to environmental covariates predict the spatial distribution of taxa and functional genes, and biodiversity data cubes aggregate occurrence records on standard spatial grids \cite{oldoni2020occurrence}. These models predict each quantity separately. When the predicted quantities form a hierarchy, for example the abundances of three hydrogenase subgroups and their sum, separately fitted predictors produce subgroup values whose sum differs from the predicted total, and they assign negative abundances where the data are sparse. Forecast reconciliation addresses the same problem for hierarchical time series by adjusting independent forecasts so that they satisfy aggregation constraints \cite{wickramasuriya2019optimal}. Correlative models also lack an explicit representation of interventions. Causal models specify interventions as operations on structural equations \cite{pearl2009causality}, but an intervention on a coarse class such as ``add rain'' has several realizations with different effects, and its consequences are then defined only up to an interval \cite{spirtes2004ambiguous,vanderweele2013multiple}. Work on causal abstraction relates models of the same system at different levels of granularity \cite{rubenstein2017causal,beckers2019abstracting}, and ontologies formalize the class hierarchies across which such relations hold \cite{owl2direct2012,thomas2019gocam}. These strands have yet to be combined into an operational twin of a soil ecosystem.
 
-A digital twin for desert management therefore needs to separate three kinds of model components. Statistical predictors estimate microbial quantities from environmental covariates, and their accuracy can be tested directly on held-out sites and campaigns. A constitutive layer, $\Phic$, derives aggregate quantities from their parts according to the axioms of an ontology $\Tont$; under explicit measurement assumptions these relations hold by definition and are unaffected by any intervention. A mechanistic layer, $\Mcaus$, contains the structural equations through which water, heat, salt, grazing and inoculation change the leaf quantities. For interventions without field experiments at the transect, $\Mcaus$ encodes causal hypotheses whose coefficients come from the literature, from companion analyses and from natural experiments; its outputs are scenarios conditional on those hypotheses, and the twin must expose the provenance of every coefficient.
+A digital twin for desert management therefore needs to separate three kinds of model components. Statistical predictors estimate microbial quantities from environmental covariates, and their accuracy can be tested directly on held-out sites and campaigns. A constitutive layer, $\Phic$, derives aggregate quantities from their parts according to the axioms of an ontology $\Tont$; under explicit measurement assumptions these relations hold by definition and are unaffected by any intervention. A mechanistic layer, $\Mcaus$, contains the structural equations through which water, heat, salt, grazing and inoculation change the leaf quantities. For interventions without field experiments at the transect, $\Mcaus$ encodes causal hypotheses whose coefficients come from the literature, from companion analyses and from paired observational comparisons; its outputs are scenarios conditional on those hypotheses, and the twin must expose the provenance of every coefficient.
 
 Here we present the Empty Quarter Biodiversity Digital Twin (EQ-BioDT). EQ-BioDT harmonizes metagenomic, amplicon, geochemical and climatic data from \nSites{} sites along a 1,043 km transect of the Rub' al-Khali into a site-by-compartment state cube indexed on H3 and ERA5 grids, with explicit flags for observed and imputed values. Its predictive layer consists of ridge regressions evaluated under spatially blocked, leave-one-campaign-out and retrospective temporal holdout validation, with site-clustered uncertainty and comparisons against simpler baselines. Its constitutive layer compiles linear constraints from the hierarchy of trace-gas guilds, prokaryotic phyla, trace-gas oxidation power and soil DNA pools stated in an OWL 2 constraint module of the Rub' al-Khali ontology, and enforces them by Euclidean projection. Its mechanistic layer couples Millington--Quirk gas diffusion, a moisture-pulse resuscitation model, a reduced guild-level flux balance model solved with HiGHS, and virome and mobilome response functions in six scenario engines. We evaluate EQ-BioDT in five validation tiers, simulate six management interventions, rank six prescribed management portfolios under four objectives, and upscale dimensional $\COtwo$ and $\COtwoeq$ budgets across the corridor and basin against seven cross-desert benchmarks. Data products follow Darwin Core, B-Cubed and RO-Crate conventions \cite{wieczorek2012dwc,oldoni2020occurrence,soilandreyes2022rocrate}, and each simulation is recorded with a content-derived identifier.
 
